aa-headunit (0.1.0-3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First Pi 5 preview release. Complete wired and wireless Android Auto
    sessions real-hardware-confirmed end to end: protocol handshake,
    live video, all three audio channels, microphone, and touch
    (including two-finger multi-touch) all streaming on the physical
    display, not just in logs.
  * Full-screen GTK4 kiosk app (`usb kiosk`) with automatic reconnect
    (wired or wireless, no operator intervention after first setup),
    a settings panel (rotation, themes, renamable EQ presets, gesture
    mapping, Wi-Fi/Bluetooth provider selection, night-mode GPIO), and
    a guided credential setup wizard.
  * `packaging/setup.sh`: single-entry-point installer that collects
    the operator's certificate/private key before installing the
    package, instead of two separate manual steps.
  * Known limitations (M8 completion gate, still open): cold-boot and
    wired/wireless-switching determinism, sustained-use/soak testing,
    power-interruption/low-storage/thermal failure-mode testing, a full
    whole-application security pass, and the phone/display/audio/USB-
    radio compatibility matrix have not yet been done. See
    MILESTONE_CHECKLIST.md's M8 section and README.md for the current,
    precise status of each. Recovery steps: docs/development/
    appliance-recovery.md.

 -- Pi Auto Head Unit Contributors <noreply@example.invalid>  Wed, 26 Aug 2026 05:00:00 +0100

aa-headunit (0.1.0-2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Wired Android Auto session (USB accessory transition, TLS handshake,
    service discovery, video/audio/touch/microphone) real-hardware-
    confirmed through Milestone 4.
  * Milestone 5 in progress: dedicated unprivileged system user,
    persistent display/rotation/audio/microphone/night-mode settings,
    USB auto-discovery and reconnect loop, appliance preflight checks,
    documented recovery path, and bounded persistent journald logging.
  * Appliance kiosk boot (cage/systemd) written and packaged but not yet
    enabled by default — real-hardware testing found it can hang the
    board; see docs/development/appliance-recovery.md.

